a:0b83619e-f7a2-4cbe-ba8e-a60f21852144}}Then by lemma REF , we have {{formula:7d369424-aa46-4005-9931-7945f672849f}}where {{formula:f28bcdfb-b0c3-4a6e-a311-299ec0a237d9}} depends on {{formula:2d079f10-e805-4e2d-a762-ccd9f679e8ac}} , {{formula:27a5e6da-8722-463f-8fba-48c3e30b4f30}} and {{formula:5b73c1bf-0cf5-49c5-9e7e-07cb35c1d59d}} .If we could control {{formula:fa581a21-3704-4101-8078-8373a74cdddf}} for any {{formula:87bdeee9-275e-4fb2-b9ce-f57dd6e52c00}} in terms of {{formula:b36dafac-9d21-4702-823a-24133c345b01}} , {{formula:4ed4848f-00d8-40a8-93ea-bdd2b20f91f9}} and {{formula:f74b70c0-e876-40f1-b489-85df6330ae68}} , we would reach a contradiction. To do this, we need higher order energy estimates, which can be done as follows.For any multi-index {{formula:08c226ac-e754-4f91-8404-65cd8e3acfa8}} with {{formula:c0fc973b-7705-4217-a52a-b5a8feb720b3}} , taking {{formula:9094b766-2d41-49cc-a8e8-4e9f667e1208}} on (REF ), multiplying {{formula:2bfaae0e-9a6d-4402-af27-7f95e07e2089}} and integrating over {{formula:c6ed89ca-97c6-4c7c-919c-e1ff409c9dfb}} , we obtain {{formula:4c2a9de8-8413-489f-b289-85aace626387}}For {{formula:4570f617-ade7-4999-869c-2a562e249731}} , we need to use the following estimate (see ) {{formula:703c6c80-c0cf-479e-94d2-c12384f87f24}}Setting {{formula:342016c6-666f-4ae3-a10d-07a58c469777}} and {{formula:febe1e35-9f17-4743-ad4d-1afc5432a331}} , we have {{formula:100a0347-8129-48ba-8954-a5d0f44d410d}}Applying the Leibniz's rule and Nirenberg's interpolation inequality, we have {{formula:c392da77-dea1-4879-af7f-4277499cfcc4}}Combining (REF ), (REF ), with (REF ), we have {{formula:2f727118-dba4-4946-a1c6-1a45d2b0d79d}}Taking {{formula:c48005f2-6643-4af0-bcf9-af57a6848430}} on (), multiplying {{formula:3b25cc20-8f02-4686-9451-14b414b941f3}} and integrating over {{formula:df6a00b0-0e31-40d5-8f86-b0d29971d2a3}} , we obtain {{formula:cf832ff0-c9e3-463f-9697-13ecee5cbb40}}For {{formula:b8b46805-f22e-427b-99dc-25109c7ab309}} , similar as the proof of (REF ), we have {{formula:b0bf8fc3-b51d-4539-82a6-f351d35672e0}}where {{formula:a93b6a6b-f9dd-490b-b11b-cef210b05a6f}} will be chosen below. Since {{formula:f694d8d9-215c-4313-a5b7-86819d898ba7}} , we have {{formula:9fe40028-42d1-4ce3-9278-01b405e2f294}}Combining (REF ), (REF ), with (REF ), we have {{formula:4ce54819-13b9-4a87-907d-0a85e22b1736}}Combining (REF ) with (REF ), we have {{formula:86701276-2bbc-476c-9d6c-6ecd04d4a309}}We can prove similar inequalities for all {{formula:4f4e0f4f-5662-4f02-b741-f9969819ccb5}} . Summing over all {{formula:2fbc78b6-0be6-4677-a7e9-c40c0640570d}} with {{formula:6e61d2ed-1fb9-4a26-9a11-8c9afcf1a1da}} , and taking {{formula:cd4785ec-44ba-4ca0-8a71-9118e2c7717d}} small enough, we have {{formula:5b5cb4e0-2393-40b9-ae77-d9b719875c75}}We now end our argument as follows. Set {{formula:ed5c907d-3bd7-43a9-8727-ee5a3343ad80}}Then by (REF ), we have {{formula:9e76df3f-e6e2-4897-b223-3a4a9a83c5d8}}By Gronwall's inequality, we obtain, {{formula:ea548f0d-15b9-480c-a108-ec753d432e23}}By combining the following critical Sobolev embedding inequality (see for the detail) {{formula:7156a280-b445-41a4-8758-e8c1b68db4b5}}with (REF ), we have {{formula:c271d6e6-c53c-4c4e-a391-0a68f158328b}}Combining this inequality with (REF ) and the ineqaulity {{formula:99d55046-8e74-43fc-b522-d3c605deb64a}} , we have, {{formula:e49a461c-bcb9-4e9b-b3b3-191c50f365ba}}By Gronwall's equality, we have {{formula:4133e2cf-6455-483a-bc2e-531d99e3dfb3}}Or equivalently, {{formula:56bfa342-e563-4a43-af2f-03d6703497f5}}for any {{formula:4b721d14-9a6c-4fc7-b6e5-424313d8f78e}} . This completes the proof.{{formula:b6a3a79f-9a39-4069-81da-99b0423c2b52}}Proof of Corollary REF: Assume that (REF ) were not true. Then there is {{formula:012673e7-d454-4a2d-9470-bdabc8e92a49}} such that {{formula:02ba9922-53c1-4814-b491-e7270a8af521}}Then lemma REF implies {{formula:74495ace-70a8-4ff6-b3e2-0ee28fbb42e4}}where {{formula:50768f91-c27e-4ec7-ab37-267fa5bdcc61}} depends only on {{formula:4cc8e537-5fec-47ca-9f26-d858de9d3fd4}} , {{formula:316bc401-8093-4e7d-b3fe-87138f41c616}} and {{formula:a794f545-042c-446c-b019-33674aa12b9f}} . In particular, we have {{formula:60c1ae02-e122-4335-91ab-015c7d762293}}On the other hand, since {{formula:96088aa1-1130-465f-ab08-6f5e14013713}} satisfies (1.5), the following energy inequality holds (cf. ): {{formula:33a215fb-1fa2-4137-8ec5-ce932493b589}}for any {{formula:34b208da-87ea-4264-9e98-a7f8ef287ff6}} . Therefore, we have {{formula:a6cf9cbe-8038-4747-8d7c-6692d641d3a6}}Applying the regularity theorem 1.2 of , we conclude {{formula:ab43967a-5743-4847-ac16-0060043d75d1}} . This contradicts the assumption that {{formula:0ecff7d9-777f-49d5-b804-2b45fa19a304}} is the first singular time. The proof is complete. {{formula:e6eaadc1-4959-4512-ab57-f59ee9621919}}", "bridgeAnswerPaperText": "First 13241 characters: Global Strong and Weak Solutions to Nematic Liquid Crystal Flow in Two Dimensions\nabstract: We consider the strong and weak solutions to the Cauchy problem of the inhomogeneous incompressible nematic liquid crystal equations in two dimensions. We first establish the local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions by using the standard domain expanding method, and then extend such local strong solution to be a global one, provided the initial density is away from vacuum and the initial direction field satisfies some geometric structure. The size of the initial data can be large. Based on such global existence results of strong solutions, by using compactness argument, we obtain the global existence of weak solutions with nonnegative initial density.\nIntroduction: The evolution of liquid crystals in {{formula:78eb628d-6438-4bfd-93b4-a74dfc50f745}} is described by the following system {{formula:6169fd2f-609f-42fa-a7bb-1d4e26d0703d}}where {{formula:054c08cc-3135-41a2-b2ac-a5b5fbe477e5}} is the density, {{formula:f9926873-9f42-4c93-bfca-83f4bd7681d7}} represents the velocity field of the flow, {{formula:af017462-57a2-4d14-a136-c8070bc9a861}} is the unit vector field that represents the macroscopic molecular orientation of the liquid crystal material and {{formula:71baa557-fafe-4495-a541-4d37631d7fd5}} denotes the pressure function. The notation {{formula:7ac8d297-767f-484a-82de-1d5f4cf6ceab}} is a {{formula:fdf0608f-2f0e-4b8d-939b-b85454ff2e3f}} matrix whose {{formula:f7ab3d88-a2cb-42f5-afe3-d01d6d7a6276}} -the entry is {{formula:4b26b9e9-64ce-4fcc-b0fe-fe13174a0736}} , {{formula:66e8bb97-ff0b-4f6e-84c0-9a34d831e07d}} .System (REF )–() is a simplified version of the Ericksen-Leslie model, which reduces to the Ossen-Frank model in the static case, for the hydrodynamics of nematic liquid crystals developed by Ericksen , and Leslie in the 1960's. Both the full Ericksen-Leslie model and the simplified version are the macroscopic continuum description of the time evolution of the materials under the influence of both the flow velocity field {{formula:20c0c4bc-74bb-40af-a9d4-45bdfe78e5c0}} and the microscopic orientation configurations {{formula:32d2b5bd-33f3-4503-a356-1030cf1ab33d}} of rod-like liquid crystals. A brief account of the Ericksen-Leslie theory and the derivations of several approximate systems can be found in the appendix of . For more details of physics, we refer the readers to the two books of Gennes-Prost and Chandrasekhar . Though the above system is a simplified version of the full Ericksen-Leslie system, it still remains the most important mathematical structures as well as most of the essential difficulties of the original Ericksen-Leslie system.In the homogeneous case, i.e. {{formula:d6325e8a-4132-40eb-abed-d850d629d2ac}} , Lin-Lin , initiated the mathematical analysis of ()–() in the 1990's. More precisely, they proved in the global existence of weak solutions to the initial and boundary value problem to the system ()–() with {{formula:be3864b8-ebfc-4cad-a2b4-5d9ead0cb27a}} replaced by Ginzburg-Landau type approximation term {{formula:d6ca588a-7bf6-4782-a01c-f574df0a53b8}} in bounded domain of two or three dimensions. They also obtain the unique existence of global classical solutions in dimension two or in dimension three but with large enough viscous coefficients. In , they proved the partial regularity theorem for suitable weak solutions, similar to the classical theorem by Caffarelli-Kohn-Nirenberg for the Navier-Stokes equation. When take the term {{formula:e272ccae-2181-47c3-b76a-8cb9d28de753}} into consideration, the system becomes more complicated from the mathematical point of view, since it is a supercritical term in the equations of {{formula:bfbc4809-cc93-41e8-83d5-2de3be58797a}} . So far, the global existence of weak solutions are proven only for the two dimensional case, see Lin, Lin and Wang , Hong and Hong and Xin . The approach used in and that used in , are different, where the global existence is proven directly to the liquid crystal system with term {{formula:27991f55-5a71-4a16-905b-4e7716a175a8}} , while in , , the strategy is to show the convergence of the solutions to the approximate system with penalty term {{formula:476e528a-8eba-4c97-ac70-80435af415f2}} as {{formula:f232bf22-a4da-4304-9011-1b4440cde4f0}} . The uniqueness of such weak solution was later proven in .In non-homogeneous case, i.e. the density dependent case, the global existence of weak solutions to the liquid crystals equations with penalty term {{formula:8ffeb3d1-d687-4e4c-b3f1-1481c4f9dee5}} instead of {{formula:82b5bccb-6bf2-4fbc-a853-a95db69875b3}} is established by Jiang and Tan in and Liu and Zhang in , see Liu and Hao and Wang and Yu for the compressible case. The existence of weak solution to density dependent liquid crystals equations with term {{formula:c652ef8b-0394-4585-8ba5-2f4dc3a9c180}} for arbitrary initial data is not known in the present, see Jiang, Jiang and Wang for a result in this direction, where the global existence of weak solution to compressible liquid crystal equations in two dimensions is obtained under some geometric assumption on the initial direction, see also Wu and Tan for the global existence of low energy weak solutions in three dimensions. When the initial data gains more regularity, one can expect to obtain more regular solutions than the weak ones. Wen and Ding obtain the local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions to the Dirichlet problem of the system (REF )–() in bounded domain with initial density being allowed to have vacuum. They also established the global existence and uniqueness of solutions for two dimensional case if the initial density is away from vacuum and the initial data is of small norm. Global existence of strong solutions with small initial data to three dimensional liquid crystal equations are obtained by Li and Wang in for constant density case, Li and Wang in for nonconstant but positive density case, and Ding, Huang and Xia in and Li for nonnegative density case.In the present paper, we consider the global existence of strong and weak solutions to the Cauchy problem of the system (REF )–(). More precisely, if the initial data is regular and away from vacuum, we obtain the global strong solutions, and if vacuum appears initially, then we obtain the global weak solutions. As the first step of our procedure, recalling the local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions has been proven in in bounded domain, we use the standard domain expanding method to obtain the local existence of strong solution to the Cauchy problem under the assumption that the initial density is away from zero. After obtaining the local strong solutions, the next step is to extend such local strong solution to be a global one. For this purpose, the main issue is to do the estimates on the local strong solutions which guarantees to extend the local solution to any finite time, obtaining the global strong solution. The energy inequalities established in Lemma REF shows that all the higher order estimates are based on space time {{formula:d0ff6b9c-96b7-4ec4-9a1a-c67b99d27f9e}} bound of {{formula:546461cb-abe5-40bc-944e-82a339dcdaad}} . Using the rigidity theorem established recently by Lei, Li and Zhang in , we can successfully obtain the a priori bound on {{formula:c901a0be-4830-4c42-a608-3a2cacd0a5ca}} norm of {{formula:78ba2c71-c9b4-4899-bfd0-38a7b2e130d6}} in space and time, and thus finish the proof of global strong solutions. And finally, using the compactness results of Lions in , we can establish the global existence of weak solutions to the Cauchy problem.Before stating our main results, we give the definitions of strong and weak solutions.Definition 1.1 Let {{formula:2209ff82-23c1-4e4d-a38d-c74b59a63a8e}} . {{formula:fb52e8f7-f695-425d-af09-d2ee376a9d0e}} is called a strong solution to the system (REF )–() in {{formula:e6868b63-9489-46f8-81a5-5db0cedbfbf0}} with initial data {{formula:de57d9ee-d261-4043-94b1-e344ec4e0a9b}} , if {{formula:3f487a90-77ce-441e-a814-ef81e108bed9}}for {{formula:65761f3c-5765-45f6-ba08-cbbbfecab39d}} , satisfies equations (REF )–() a.e. in {{formula:052ef63c-fa40-48e2-b5f1-7cf78ac7c2af}} , and the initial data {{formula:dcd2cbf4-4283-4678-9d2e-cedf97b0f1a9}} a.e. in {{formula:9b3b6016-4258-465f-bd21-8b0d46d6546a}} .Definition 1.2 Let {{formula:bef7044d-51e0-458e-aef5-73ba470cf77e}} . A triple {{formula:646f76fc-b9f1-471d-a3d1-e0247c84000a}} is called a weak solution to the system (REF )–() {{formula:f4d86bb9-d20e-4c3f-be9a-372944c75290}} with initial data {{formula:0624de37-12e7-4ce4-9d7f-cf2ee943bc05}} , if {{formula:5467349f-d73a-4c1a-bd5f-801dd737cacd}}and the following hold true {{formula:67d4f101-de67-49e5-8bc1-e0d1de4eaced}}for all {{formula:8d39f0ab-728d-41a8-aec8-7a25e1aec51e}} , {{formula:8be22fc7-f100-4eed-a55b-535726e53160}} with {{formula:ceb7973e-3ada-4db1-9277-b137125e18a0}} and {{formula:a78ce5b2-f27e-41af-b384-5b74cda2de23}} .Our main results are stated in the following two theorems.Theorem 1.1 Assume that {{formula:a0b12f9f-ecf5-4497-99a4-ccdadad0f1c1}} , {{formula:7a316116-88a7-4125-ba56-e613aa9b7e4b}} , {{formula:d21ae1ea-020d-4246-9688-7043a659eff4}} , {{formula:f2a8c228-d600-4125-8451-1b918bfe9619}} with {{formula:40ef6f6e-ff6b-4142-bbbe-f287ea8ad2bc}} , {{formula:793904ff-7fa1-49e6-b6ac-c25fe33f3ea1}} and {{formula:27f39d71-15e5-43ed-a6be-a5cf46d3cf55}} , where {{formula:32af244c-cc83-47c4-bd09-271aa6ebc65d}} , {{formula:55fb32c7-6b6d-481f-8987-4766601f0eb7}} and {{formula:3b160cc7-878c-4492-8ba0-f03b9a5f6963}} are positive constants. Then there exists a unique global strong solution {{formula:a537b481-d393-496c-9267-9e085f250ec3}} to the system (REF )–(), complemented with the initial data {{formula:3e579793-5d43-4912-ab58-3f0e0b60b666}} .Theorem 1.2 Assume that {{formula:edeb19ee-e542-46b7-a448-b0a5037fff25}} , {{formula:c92d4780-b8aa-4745-810d-2639706f39f9}} for some {{formula:dc162fcb-6ef8-42cd-8bca-f953e981e6fb}} , {{formula:4d8cedc5-694a-4fad-84d1-134797664a37}} , {{formula:b04402a8-90f1-4509-a84e-37bba5f6b1a9}} with {{formula:191a3246-eb35-4c1f-a56c-b0aef4d509b7}} , {{formula:754f1a0b-c800-4ebe-b393-b08b21caaf40}} and {{formula:bfd8520f-ba75-45a9-a9f8-b6383544b999}} , where {{formula:a575dab5-56e9-4530-839a-f452ede1d445}} , {{formula:0172a11a-5284-4fcf-88ea-485d367de7f1}} and {{formula:ed6f6a12-7217-4a00-bbb9-c417708f6d74}} are positive constants, {{formula:7696e9dc-713b-423c-a660-15953ddb625e}} is the third component of the vector {{formula:be80bae3-752a-436b-ae35-641ebe2a0a0e}} . Then there exists a global weak solution {{formula:99bcbf19-e166-4ec0-94f2-1b66e15b7846}} to the system (REF )–(), complemented with the initial data {{formula:9b971b0a-a115-47d0-9a2f-404dd050a336}} .Remark 1.1 In theorem REF we assume that the initial density is away from vacuum, while in Theorem REF , the initial density is allowed to have vacuum.The rest of this paper is arranged as follows: In section , we do some estimates on the local strong solutions in the balls {{formula:480edc81-3f2c-4543-ba6b-cb2cce3fb2fa}} , including the a priori energy estimates and the existence time of strong solutions independent of the parameter {{formula:6b7552c0-a49f-4f54-9f4d-8ed19b1513f7}} ; In section , by taking the limit {{formula:07755ca2-cd03-450c-86f3-dc4d18eed167}} of the solutions obtained in the previous section, we firstly establish the local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions to the Cauchy problem of liquid crystal equations via the standard domain expanding argument, and then extend such local strong solution to be a global one; and finally, in section , we obtain the global weak solution by using compactness argument.\nEstimates on the local strong solutions in bounded domains: In this section, as preparations of the next section, we do some estimates on the local strong solutions in the balls {{formula:4cae27ab-8ff0-4da7-94b4-4ee7e0745be7}} , including the a priori energy estimates and the existence time of strong solutions independent of the parameter {{formula:530e5c2c-4913-42fe-b7c4-9d9202844c0d}} .We first state the following local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions to the Dirichlet problem in bounded domain.Lemma 2.1 (Local existence in bounded domain, see ) Assume that {{formula:8679bc31-0a9a-4716-8906-3bd622e048d4}} , {{formula:d5d60e22-3fb2-4f08-bc20-c7c1764b009c}} , {{formula:d6613508-bc9e-497d-8db4-6f6f8e3668ee}} , {{formula:b4414eb2-becd-4950-bd0d-bbedfe1b3741}} with {{formula:f38220f9-56f5-4766-a333-36ad59eea879}} , {{formula:65f24db3-8f7e-4844-b695-35fa25800222}} on {{formula:bd7e8ca6-ae64-41f0-bdaa-89686b44a800}} , and the following compatible condition is valid {{formula:f3bf1209-5003-4f5e-b45b-40e4447b0220}}for {{formula:ed005576-6ef3-4151-bcd4-de59d99145fe}} . Then there exists a constant {{formula:4b2577d7-a8f5-4ec6-94a5-885a58bfd2e4}} , such that the system (REF )–() complemented with the initial and boundary conditions {{formula:6d5744f8-f7a2-45cf-9bb3-f510a17d8845}}has a unique solution {{formula:ff5f57b8-24aa-4c24-a6be-16462324fb94}} on {{formula:9353ae1d-0bf2-4a90-81b5-b8be64cde77f}} , satisfying {{formula:c4f8fdf6-ea87-4c61-a67d-45a8d1d7fd7c}}Now, we state and   Last 13241 characters: REF )–(REF ), by taking {{formula:5a3b1aaa-6576-40cf-8ee4-e7d4291aedb8}} small enough, there holds {{formula:ede6161d-e5bc-4cbf-b2b8-3397034649e1}}Taking the operator {{formula:2027dfe6-c13e-4c1e-a465-f544b89722a8}} on both sides of equation (REF ), multiplying the resulting equation by {{formula:44ae188a-7ee3-43eb-a038-78de0e23d6be}} and integrating over {{formula:c32d84d3-d113-4391-abf4-f068b1ff3778}} yields {{formula:691ee867-8732-4ab5-8eac-e425b1c09a69}}and thus {{formula:f91440f7-9d0f-4174-9d57-ef2ff5ac94a5}}By the aid of this estimate, it follows from equation (REF ) that {{formula:5ab8c077-c44c-4768-8b95-051d084b0c7b}}The proof is complete.\nLocal and global strong solutions of Cauchy problem: In this section, we prove the global existence and uniqueness of strong solutions to the Cauchy problem of the system (REF )–(), i.e. we will give the proof of Theorem REF . Our strategy is firstly proving the local existence and uniqueness of strong solutions and then extending the local solution to be a global one.We need the following compactness lemma.Lemma 3.1 (See Simon Corollary 4) Assume that {{formula:7fafa112-7017-4ce5-9062-356abfe3a211}} and {{formula:1e964ada-4053-462c-93a0-db556e59606e}} are three Banach spaces, with {{formula:a7d0ef62-4e66-4039-b70b-039347c69b35}} Then the following hold true(i) If {{formula:5b552ed9-7590-4eb2-973a-1efcad93fcf8}} is a bounded subset of {{formula:fec343d2-9e63-402f-93b4-c323ba1e35e3}} where {{formula:f52e0575-12a2-4011-a296-c8e50d96d8cc}} , and {{formula:aeb913d8-b776-4b10-b9c3-719fe50ee023}} is bounded in {{formula:f88ce5da-974a-4b43-89f2-f8c5a1e0ccac}} . Then {{formula:b0486865-3aaa-4e23-a797-8ad9eec058e2}} is relatively compact in {{formula:95c5ae6a-4450-463f-8ea9-ce0f249267b1}} ;(ii) If {{formula:53f6fa00-c49d-4bf7-864f-d754cda625d6}} is bounded in {{formula:256d4c95-1c78-4ace-970a-e64c6c851d45}} and {{formula:12eaed86-9b97-43d5-9fd0-2c191bab8e48}} is bounded in {{formula:0a6d1668-51f3-4dac-a9f2-c8002ffbcaee}} where {{formula:a48d951d-449f-47e4-8a90-3f7f9c738c2a}} . Then {{formula:be8a90f2-45ac-4c75-a855-20c7aa222050}} is relatively compact in {{formula:f98428cd-7be9-405e-bc5b-f2233abdc96f}} .Our local existence and uniqueness result is stated and proven in the following proposition.Proposition 3.1 (Local strong solution) Assume that {{formula:b02af80b-89dd-4243-80a4-852fe77b71dd}} , {{formula:98557de9-bcf1-42e2-a14a-7c76a0562d66}} , {{formula:0368a6e6-44c6-446c-be24-9bed7fd7e72f}} with {{formula:d99572bf-f9db-4119-aea5-9ebad2e49fa7}} , {{formula:0f645182-7ea5-40d0-9a17-f9d15176ee3d}} with {{formula:4744d0f6-b048-4dd1-a432-5527bfb905b8}} .Then there exists a time {{formula:542f59c8-4a45-4682-96d7-104d0fa20eeb}} depending only on {{formula:ae41033a-6915-48df-8d3b-6d99f23f154a}} , {{formula:5c4b3aa4-1c3c-41af-adc2-a6e5f374c72a}} , {{formula:3afb20a3-4310-4b83-986c-9e4d5fef50d1}} and {{formula:89a4652c-a7ca-4255-bf79-fab45e1a6f87}} , such that the system (REF )–() complemented with the initial data {{formula:9f58850b-593a-4842-b284-61c17e1ea6e9}} has a unique strong solution {{formula:96abaa76-5b5e-4200-81d7-e7506a59c52a}} in {{formula:d41333e3-2fe2-4430-a626-dec2689a97ee}} , satisfying {{formula:ff51ab59-bffb-42c2-bf64-c642b8eb48b4}}for {{formula:6c744688-9d92-4be4-ab99-c4650a8ba7c9}} .Since {{formula:d3e44373-297f-45ca-ba38-c2051373bcb8}} , {{formula:2bdc11f8-aad0-4c8f-a5da-97a48beb80b0}} , there is a sequence {{formula:d2bd35c1-aeb1-4571-a73c-506efb9cc274}} , with {{formula:5b3df5f6-df3d-4657-a452-1c29169e5115}} and {{formula:011730bd-f2dc-475c-a5e3-b37fe99354c9}} and {{formula:9accd914-8c7a-4071-99a2-b53e3e0db583}} , such that {{formula:49f83274-9cbb-4162-86a0-ee28a7ae3f1e}} in {{formula:1417de9b-db78-4e33-a763-b20f9e636b1c}} . Since {{formula:49668837-d3fe-458c-af2e-5f55d6a3db1f}} , the compatible condition in Lemma REF holds true with {{formula:35bf5042-d887-490f-8e23-d43011da67f7}} . Let {{formula:943f4375-190e-4c32-b188-656293b2c04c}} be the constants stated in Lemma REF with {{formula:dfa2cb72-45ac-48a2-b567-7a5821ddb14a}} replaced by {{formula:76fe30f9-0fbf-4ed0-8b7c-c26e25be468a}} . Recalling that {{formula:55316924-f680-4442-b564-4e2d4f4aed94}} in {{formula:6fbebc5d-5f72-4900-90ca-514c923d2b47}} , then one can easily estimate {{formula:951ce456-735f-476e-94d1-037ad4c648d9}} .By Lemma REF and Lemma REF , there is a time {{formula:25f54e7b-1f55-4584-a0e6-00728c1cf279}} depending only on {{formula:c01bde21-dd6d-49f8-9675-a2e9a7b56e2e}} , {{formula:6b590560-6c1e-4c08-88fa-074a98ee8a50}} , {{formula:303b4e02-bbeb-4b31-9095-8c532a0b9301}} , {{formula:08a52965-c09b-4fc5-884a-37e66d2becd2}} , such that the system (REF )–() has a unique strong solution {{formula:aee24830-fd34-4a53-9f48-bd0535366a3e}} in {{formula:0fd6b894-4021-430f-95bc-3fc10f72f8de}} , complemented with the initial data {{formula:fa7b26ac-20a8-4894-906e-244b820cfb9f}} and the boundary value data {{formula:bf39bda0-d0d4-42ef-b646-e92790f020b9}} , and there hold {{formula:ca4a73d0-3fc9-419d-8a9b-a78d65fe3303}}for {{formula:eeb71802-6b45-4033-a83a-2a0a115eb8c5}} . On account of this estimates, using diagonal argument, there is a subsequence, still indexed by {{formula:ccd40cd2-de5d-40a1-9ed4-14a03a3fe114}} , such that {{formula:3fe91db3-621c-4e12-8ce8-ab79adb8b357}}for any {{formula:80150224-24ca-4582-b4a0-e9ba073fff45}} , and the following inequality holds true {{formula:76b37970-174b-4257-830e-a9f10ffae10e}}By the Lemma REF , there holds {{formula:898e3109-ac10-48ea-909d-ced7f47b8b6c}}for any {{formula:b0353159-8e17-4fb5-93ae-c0cab376f958}} .On account of all the above convergence, we can take the limit {{formula:ca1feb60-d0c2-4db3-9549-f21a92501993}} to conclude that {{formula:33c2959a-0713-43f5-acc1-fb2dc6407a61}} is a strong solution to the system (REF )–() complemented with the initial data {{formula:f509c8ed-5a1b-48a8-b53d-6bf5b131fded}} . The uniqueness can be proven in the standard way, thus we omit it here. The proof is complete.We also need the following rigidity theorem, which grantees the a priori bound on the {{formula:e8673ed0-dbce-4e6c-990e-90391f443533}} space time norm of {{formula:f10e5122-87c4-4320-b7e5-41787f7cb522}} .Lemma 3.2 (See ) Let {{formula:9d913b2f-a1b0-4c27-b60e-b83a605dbfb6}} and {{formula:4343cb5b-595b-456e-b5b3-8b2ad5306822}} . There exists a positive constant {{formula:e41ca116-ee37-49b8-8e50-03619db03675}} such that the following holds:If {{formula:cad0f5d1-bfa1-42f8-88b0-702769f62840}} , {{formula:42abd822-0255-4b5a-bc11-060961cac233}} with {{formula:7d1d5c05-9336-4a78-a5bd-a926207b8b3e}} and {{formula:66ea2c53-f445-4aa3-becc-49434e480109}} , where {{formula:1fbd02d2-947e-4d36-be98-ac3055c31244}} is the third component of the vector {{formula:037b999b-b992-4629-b02b-824eecca27c9}} , then {{formula:5194662a-24f5-46d4-aabf-c7f1f2a0f810}}Consequently, for such maps the associated harmonic energy is coercive, i.e., {{formula:14a6caec-89a6-479f-98ee-5c6d55c9d694}}Now, we can give the proof of Theorem REF as follows.Proof of Theorem REF . By Proposition REF , there is a unique strong solution {{formula:438c3fe0-ee75-45e5-8681-9b852bd23144}} in {{formula:e595b945-adaa-47d9-a765-fedb4b036cb5}} . To show the global existence, we only need to extend such local solution to be a global one. For this purpose, it suffices to show that the quantity {{formula:859adfc3-aea5-4657-ac9a-51cda3c73725}}is finite for any {{formula:4d8bc2a0-d3d0-439d-8df3-1c382c243e06}} .We extend the local strong solution to the maximal time {{formula:a7b5d31d-8892-4b5a-ab98-aa984a3d223c}} . Using the same argument in Lemma REF , we can prove that {{formula:3020450d-f00f-4cbd-b00e-17ac4fb68743}}for any {{formula:05799ee7-614c-4d80-bbd2-4333b05639c3}} , where {{formula:85bf8955-f74f-40f8-b208-87b0590ad5ea}} is a positive constant depending only on {{formula:84b99988-9ca5-47e2-84d2-ba3c5654a28c}} , {{formula:97